===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|abyen}}, {{m|enm|abien}}, {{m|enm|abiggen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ābyċġan||to buy; pay for; buy off; requite; recompense; redeem; perform; execute}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*uzbugjaną}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|a|buy}}. Cognate with {{cog|got|𐌿𐍃𐌱𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽}}.<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=11}}</ref> Not related to {{m|en|abide}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|en|/əˈbaɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-aby.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪ|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{head|en|verb|simple past and past participle|abought|no other forms attested in Modern English}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[pay#Verb|pay]] the [[penalty]] for (something); to [[atone]] for, to [[make amends]]. {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|make up}}
##* {{RQ:Beaumont Knight|sig=G|page=48|passage=Foole-hardy Knight, full soone thou shalt '''aby''' / This fond reproach, thy body will I hang, [''Hee takes down / his pole.''] / And loe vpon that string thy teeth shall hang: Prepare thy selfe, for dead soone shalt thou bee}}
## {{lb|en|archaic|figuratively}} To pay (something) as a penalty, to [[atone]] for; to [[suffer]] (something). {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|sustain}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=III|canto=IV|stanza=38|page=455|passage=Who dyes the vtmoſt dolor doth '''abye''', / But who that liues, is lefte to waile his loſſe: / So life is loſſe, and death felicity.}}
##* {{RQ:Morris Earthly Paradise|part=IV|page=339|passage=We doubt thee not; / Thy tale seems true, nor dost thou glorify / Thyself herein—certes thou wouldst '''abye''' / A heavy fate if thou shouldst lie herein— {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|except|Scotland}} To [[endure]] or [[tolerate]] (something); to [[experience]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}<ref name=DSL>{{R:DSL|pos=v}}</ref>
##: {{synonyms|en|brook|go on|hold on|put up with|Thesaurus:persist|Thesaurus:tolerate}}
##* {{RQ:Stevenson Catriona|chapter=The Wood by Silvermills|page=131|passage=The muckle black deil was father to the Frasers, a'body kens that; and as for the Gregara, I never could '''abye''' the reek of them since I could stotter on two feet.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To pay for (something); to [[buy#Verb|buy]]. {{defdate|12th–16th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|procure|purchase|Thesaurus:buy}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}}
## To pay the penalty; to atone. {{defdate|12th–16th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|expiate|propitiate}}
## To endure; to [[remain#Verb|remain]]. {{defdate|14th–16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=III|canto=VII|stanza=3|pages=494–495|pageref=495|passage=So long as breath, and hable puiſſaunce / Did natiue corage vnto him ſupply, / His pace he freſhly forward did aduaunce, / And carried her beyond all ieopardy, / But nought that wanteth reſt, can long '''aby'''.}}

